---
# DISK AND INODE CAPACITY, PORTED FROM THE bitcashier FLEET'S filesystem-capacity.rules.
#
# WHY IT IS WORTH PORTING RATHER THAN WRITING FRESH: that fleet had a control-plane host reach 100%
# INODE exhaustion with 19G of bytes still free, and nothing fired -- it was found by hand. The
# cause was accumulated NixOS system generations with nothing collecting the store, which is a
# property of NixOS rather than of that fleet, so this fleet inherits the exposure the day it
# boots. The mongo host is the one to watch: a database volume grows monotonically and cannot be
# collected, so its bytes alert is a CAPACITY warning (grow the volume) rather than a cleanup one.
#
# INODES FIRST, AND THAT ORDERING IS THE POINT. A bytes-only alert -- the one everybody writes --
# watches the whole of an inode exhaustion happen and stays silent. ENOSPC has two causes and
# `df -h` shows one of them. At zero inodes a machine still routes traffic and answers health
# checks while every process that tries to CREATE a file fails: it looks alive from outside and
# cannot write a log line.
#
# WHY `mountpoint!="/nix/store"` -- and this one is inherited as a MEASUREMENT rather than a guess:
# on the fleet this was ported from, every NixOS host reports `/` and `/nix/store` as two series
# with IDENTICAL values, because /nix/store is a bind mount of the root filesystem. Without the
# exclusion every alert here fires TWICE for one filesystem, and the duplicate reads as two
# machines in trouble rather than one. Worth re-checking on this fleet the first time one of these
# fires; the exclusion costs nothing if the bind mount is ever gone.
#
# THRESHOLDS: inodes warn at 20% and page at 8%; bytes warn at 20% and page at 7%. The two
# warnings match deliberately — "a fifth left" is one number to remember for both axes, and the
# bytes one was 15% until 2026-09-05 for no reason that had been written down.
#
# WHY PROPORTIONS AND NOT FLOORS. A gigabyte free is roomy on a 40G root and nearly nothing on the
# Mongo volume, so a byte floor is either noise on the small disks or blindness on the big ones.
#
# POST-MIGRATION AUDIT (2026-08-29): EVERY RULE IN THIS FILE IS KEPT AS WRITTEN. It was written
# against node_exporter on these three Hetzner hosts, so nothing in it assumed a Fly-shaped world
# and nothing in it needed rewriting. Two things DID change around it:
#
#   * It now covers the two mountpoints that the Grafana rule "Disk space low (persistent volume)"
#     used to watch on Fly (`/data/db` and `/var/lib/grafana` on Fly volumes). Those machines no
#     longer exist and that rule is dropped where it lives, in
#     grafana/alerting/alert-rules.yaml. The equivalent volumes are now /var/lib/mongodb and
#     /var/lib/monitoring, and they are covered here by the generic mountpoint match rather than by
#     name -- which is better, because a mount nobody thought to list is watched by default.
#   * `FilesystemWillFillWithin7Days` at the bottom is NEW, and is the rewrite of the other dropped
#     Fly rule ("Disk projected to fill within 7 days"). See its own comment.
#
# WHY tmpfs AND ramfs ARE EXCLUDED AND NOTHING ELSE IS. They are memory, not disk, and several sit
# permanently at 0% free by construction -- systemd's 1MB `/run/credentials/*` mounts are full the
# moment they exist. The filter is an EXCLUDE list rather than an allow-list of ext4/xfs on
# purpose: a filesystem type nobody thought of must default to WATCHED and cost a false page, never
# default to unwatched and cost a silent one.
groups:
  - name: filesystem-capacity
    rules:
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # INODES. The axis that actually bit the fleet this was ported from.
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: FilesystemInodesLow
        expr: >-
          node_filesystem_files_free{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"}
            / node_filesystem_files{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"} < 0.20
        # HALF AN HOUR, because inode counts move in steps rather than drifting: a deploy or a
        # large unpack lands thousands at once. Long enough that a build which creates and removes
        # a tree does not page; short enough to name a host days before it hits the wall.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Fewer than 20% of the inodes on {{ $labels.mountpoint }} ({{ $labels.host }}) are free"
          description: >-
            This is not the same as running out of space and `df -h` will not show it. On a NixOS
            host the usual cause is accumulated system generations and store paths: check
            `df -i /` and `ls /nix/var/nix/profiles/ | grep -c system-`, then collect with
            `nix-collect-garbage --delete-older-than 14d`. If that has already run, find the real
            consumer with `for d in /var /root /tmp; do echo $d $(find $d -xdev | wc -l); done`.

      - alert: FilesystemInodesCritical
        expr: >-
          node_filesystem_files_free{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"}
            / node_filesystem_files{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"} < 0.08
        # TEN MINUTES. Past here the remaining headroom is a single large deploy, and the repair
        # gets HARDER rather than easier as it runs out -- see the description.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "Fewer than 8% of the inodes on {{ $labels.mountpoint }} ({{ $labels.host }}) are free"
          description: >-
            ACT NOW. Past zero the host serves traffic while nothing on it can create a file, and
            `nix-collect-garbage` cannot even take its own lock ("opening lock file ... No space
            left on device") -- so the ordinary fix stops working at exactly the moment it is
            needed. Break that deadlock by removing old generation SYMLINKS first, which needs no
            lock: keep the ten most recent /nix/var/nix/profiles/system-*-link, clear the dangling
            /nix/var/nix/gcroots/auto entries, then run nix-collect-garbage.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # BYTES. The ordinary axis, and still worth having -- it is simply not the one that bit.
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: FilesystemSpaceLow
        expr: >-
          node_filesystem_avail_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"}
            / node_filesystem_size_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"} < 0.20
        # 20%, RAISED FROM 15% ON 2026-09-05, and matching the inode warning above rather than
        # sitting five points under it for no reason anybody could state. The whole fleet had
        # room when it changed — the tightest filesystem was mongo-1's data volume at 73.5% free
        # — so nothing fired on the way in, which is the only moment a threshold can be moved
        # without arguing about a live alert.
        #
        # PER FILESYSTEM, NOT PER MACHINE. The series carry `host` and `mountpoint`, so a host
        # with a full data volume and a roomy root pages once, naming the mount that is full;
        # a machine-level rule would have to pick one filesystem to be wrong about. mongo-1's
        # /var/lib/mongodb is 10.5 GB, so 20% there is ~2 GB — small in bytes, and the right
        # shape anyway, because a database volume is grown deliberately rather than cleaned up.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Less than 20% of {{ $labels.mountpoint }} ({{ $labels.host }}) is free"
          description: >-
            Note WHICH mountpoint. On `/` the usual cause is the nix store or an unrotated log. On
            /var/lib/mongodb it is the data itself, which grows monotonically and cannot be
            collected -- that one is a capacity decision (grow the volume) rather than a cleanup,
            and it wants planning before the critical alert rather than after. On
            /var/lib/mongodb-backups, check whether rotation is still working: the dump keeps the
            most recent N by count, so a growing directory means dumps are landing and none are
            being pruned. `du -xh --max-depth=2 <mount> | sort -h | tail -20` names the consumer.

      - alert: FilesystemSpaceCritical
        expr: >-
          node_filesystem_avail_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"}
            / node_filesystem_size_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"} < 0.07
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "Less than 7% of {{ $labels.mountpoint }} ({{ $labels.host }}) is free"
          description: >-
            A full data volume stops the service that owns it: mongod refuses writes and cannot
            grow its oplog, and on the monitoring host a full volume takes Prometheus AND Grafana's
            database down together. Free space or grow the volume now.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE TREND, AS OPPOSED TO THE LEVEL. This is the REWRITE of the Fly rule "Disk projected to
      # fill within 7 days", which read `fly_instance_filesystem_blocks_avail` on the Fly machines'
      # volumes and has had no data since those machines stopped. Same intent, same 7-day horizon,
      # same 6-hour fitting window; node_exporter's series instead of Fly's agent's.
      #
      # WHY A PROJECTION IS WORTH HAVING BESIDE FOUR LEVEL ALERTS. The level rules fire when the
      # disk is ALREADY nearly full, which on /var/lib/mongodb is a capacity decision -- attach a
      # bigger volume, resize the filesystem, restart mongod -- and that is a change to make on a
      # Tuesday afternoon, not at the point `FilesystemSpaceCritical` fires. A monotonically
      # growing database gives days of warning if anything is looking for the slope.
      #
      # `predict_linear` IS A STRAIGHT LINE THROUGH SIX HOURS AND IT IS A CRUDE ONE. It will predict
      # doom from an ordinary six-hour import and predict safety through a step change it has not
      # seen yet. The two-hour `for:` absorbs most of the first kind; nothing absorbs the second,
      # which is what the level alerts above are for. Both halves are needed and neither is
      # sufficient.
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: FilesystemWillFillWithin7Days
        expr: >-
          predict_linear(
            node_filesystem_avail_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"}[6h],
            7 * 24 * 3600) < 0
          and
          node_filesystem_avail_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"}
            / node_filesystem_size_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|ramfs",mountpoint!="/nix/store"} < 0.40
        # THE SECOND CLAUSE IS NOT REDUNDANT AND IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THIS FIRING USEFULLY AND
        # FIRING CONSTANTLY. On a nearly empty disk a trivial slope still extrapolates to zero
        # inside a week -- 500MB written onto a 40G root over six hours projects out to 14G, which
        # "fills" a disk that has 38G free. Requiring the filesystem to be under 60% used as well
        # means the projection is only consulted once there is something to project INTO.
        # 40% free is a guess, chosen to sit comfortably above `FilesystemSpaceLow`'s threshold
        # (15% when this was written, 20% since 2026-09-05) so the two alerts arrive in the right
        # order rather than together.
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.mountpoint }} on {{ $labels.host }} is on course to fill within 7 days"
          description: >-
            A TREND, NOT A LEVEL -- there is still room right now. On /var/lib/mongodb this is the
            alert that gives enough notice to grow the volume deliberately; on / it usually means
            the Nix store is accumulating faster than the collector removes it, so check
            `NixGarbageCollectionStale` in nixos-deploy.rules before treating it as a capacity
            problem. Straight-line extrapolation over six hours is easily fooled by a one-off bulk
            write; confirm against a longer window before acting.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE COMPANION, AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. Every rule above is a COMPARISON, and a comparison
      # cannot fire when its series is gone -- so node_exporter dying, or its filesystem collector
      # being turned off, would leave all four silent and indistinguishable from a fleet with room
      # to spare. Fleet-wide rather than per host: one host dropping out of scrape is a different
      # question, answered by `TargetDown` in monitoring-self.rules.
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: FilesystemMetricsAbsent
        expr: absent(node_filesystem_files_free) or absent(node_filesystem_avail_bytes)
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "No host is publishing filesystem metrics"
          description: >-
            None of the four capacity alerts can fire, so nothing is watching any disk or inode
            table on this fleet. Either node_exporter's filesystem collector is disabled everywhere
            or Prometheus has stopped scraping it. IT DOES NOT MEAN EVERY FILESYSTEM HAS ROOM.
